---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ElementCard from '../components/ElementCard.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const elements = (await getCollection('elements', ({ data }) => !data.draft)).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
);
---

<Base
  title="Native UI — interactive elements the platform ships"
  description="A catalogue of modern HTML and CSS interactive elements that used to need a JavaScript library and now ship in the browser."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <header class="doc-header">
      <p class="eyebrow mono">Native UI</p>
      <h1>The platform does this now.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Dialogs, popovers, anchored tooltips and carousels used to mean reaching for
        a library. They are HTML attributes and CSS pseudo-elements now. This is a
        catalogue of what has landed, what it looks like running, and where it still
        bites.
      </p>
    </header>

    <ul class="card-grid">
      {
        elements.map((entry) => (
          <ElementCard
            href={`/elements/${entry.id}/`}
            elementName={entry.data.elementName}
            description={entry.data.description}
            webFeatures={entry.data.webFeatures}
          />
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How it is built</h2>
      <p>
        Every demo is plain HTML, CSS and vanilla JavaScript — no framework runtime,
        no CSS framework, nothing to install to reproduce it. The code you read is
        the code that runs: each demo's markup and stylesheet are read from disk at
        build time, rendered into the page, and syntax-highlighted from the same
        string, so the snippet and the working example cannot drift apart.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site itself is a static Astro build. Pages come from MDX, and adding a
        new element means one content file and one folder of demo files.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the support data comes from</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing on this site is hand-typed. Baseline status comes from the
        <a href="https://github.com/web-platform-dx/web-features">web-features</a>
        dataset, per-browser versions from
        <a href="https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data">MDN browser-compat-data</a>,
        and usage share from <a href="https://caniuse.com/">caniuse</a> — all read at
        build time from the installed packages.
      </p>
      <p>
        A scheduled job refreshes those packages, rebuilds, and flags any feature
        whose status moved, which is how a page that says <em>limited availability</em>
        today stops saying it once the last browser ships.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
